      "text": "Cognitive load theory, developed by educational psychologist John Sweller in the 1980s, proposes that the human working memory has a strictly limited capacity — generally estimated at holding only four to seven items simultaneously — and that instructional design must be sensitive to this constraint if learning is to be effective. Sweller distinguished three types of cognitive load: intrinsic load, which arises from the inherent complexity of the material being learned; extraneous load, which results from poor instructional design and represents wasted mental effort; and germane load, which refers to the mental effort devoted to constructing and automating schemas — generalized mental frameworks that facilitate future learning. The central prescription of the theory is that instructors should minimize extraneous load by presenting information clearly and coherently, so that learners can devote their available cognitive resources to germane processing. For example, the split-attention effect occurs when learners must simultaneously consult two sources of information that must be mentally integrated, such as a diagram and a separate textual explanation. By physically integrating the text within the diagram, designers can reduce extraneous load and improve learning outcomes. Research in cognitive load theory has also demonstrated a worked-examples effect: novice learners benefit more from studying fully worked solutions to problems than from attempting to solve novel problems independently, because problem-solving search processes consume working memory without necessarily building schemas. As learners gain expertise and their schemas become automated, however, the advantage of worked examples diminishes — a phenomenon known as the expertise reversal effect.",
