      "id": "st-toefl_app_the_thermohaline_circulation",
      "kind": "Academic Passage",
      "title": "The Thermohaline Circulation",
      "text": "The ocean's thermohaline circulation — a vast system of interconnected currents that transports water across all the world's major oceans — plays a critical role in regulating Earth's climate. Often described as a \"global conveyor belt,\" the system moves enormous quantities of heat from the tropics toward the polar regions, significantly moderating temperatures at high latitudes that would otherwise be far more extreme. Without this oceanic heat transport, cities like London and Oslo, located at latitudes comparable to cold subarctic regions of Canada, would experience dramatically harsher winters.\n\nThe term \"thermohaline\" derives from the Greek words for heat (thermo) and salt (haline), reflecting the two physical properties that drive the circulation. Differences in water density, caused by variations in temperature and salinity, provide the energy that keeps the system in motion. Cold water is denser than warm water, and saltier water is denser than fresh water. When warm surface water traveling northward in the Atlantic cools and becomes saltier due to evaporation, it grows dense enough to sink thousands of meters to the ocean floor, driving a flow of deep, cold water southward along the ocean bottom.\n\nThis sinking of dense water near the North Atlantic — a process known as deep water formation — is a linchpin of the entire circulation system. From the North Atlantic, cold deep water flows southward along the ocean floor into the Southern Ocean and eventually into the deep basins of the Indian and Pacific Oceans. Meanwhile, surface currents carry warm tropical water northward to replace the sinking water. This cycle of sinking and surface flow has persisted for millions of years, though it has fluctuated in strength during past periods of climate change.\n\nThe consequences of thermohaline circulation for climate extend beyond simple heat transport. The circulation also plays a vital role in the ocean's ability to absorb atmospheric carbon dioxide. Deep water formation carries surface water — and the CO₂ dissolved in it — to the ocean depths, effectively sequestering carbon in the deep ocean for hundreds to thousands of years. This mechanism has been instrumental in regulating atmospheric CO₂ concentrations over geological timescales.\n\nClimate scientists are now closely monitoring the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC), a key component of the global thermohaline system, for signs of weakening. Observations over recent decades indicate that AMOC has slowed compared to its average strength over the past millennium. The leading hypothesis attributes this slowdown to the influx of freshwater from melting Greenland ice sheets, which dilutes the dense salty water in the North Atlantic and inhibits the sinking process that drives the circulation.\n\nModels projecting future climate change suggest that a significant weakening of AMOC could have severe regional consequences. For Western Europe, a substantial reduction in heat transport from the tropics could partially offset the warming expected from greenhouse gas increases, leading to complex and regionally variable climate outcomes. Some models predict that a near-collapse of AMOC could trigger abrupt regional cooling in northwestern Europe even as global average temperatures continue to rise. Historical evidence from past ice ages, when thermohaline circulation was substantially weaker, shows that such abrupt climate shifts have occurred naturally on geological timescales.",
